---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Skills for resume for freshers: what to list, what to leave out, and how to prove each one';
const seoTitle = 'Skills for Resume for Freshers: What to List and How to Prove It';
const description =
  'Skills for resume for freshers: how many to list, how to word each one with a tool, an action and proof, and what ATS really does. Examples for seven degrees.';
const seoDescription =
  'Skills for resume for freshers: how many to list, how to word each skill with a tool, an action and proof, what ATS really does, and examples for seven degrees.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/resume/skills-for-resume-for-freshers/';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-10-14';
const publishedDateLabel = '14 October 2026';
const authorName = 'Allu Vagdevi';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/allu-vagdevi/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'What to write in skills in resume: the short answer' },
  { id: 'receipt-line', label: 'The receipt line: tool, action, checkable output' },
  { id: 'resume-as-sales', label: 'Your resume is a one-page sales document' },
  { id: 'list-or-leave', label: 'What to list, what to leave out, how many' },
  { id: 'ats-truth', label: 'How ATS and recruiters treat skills' },
  { id: 'soft-skills', label: 'Soft skills and communication in English' },
  { id: 'by-background', label: 'Before and after lines by degree' },
  { id: 'core-multiplier', label: 'Core skill, multiplier skill and proof shelf' },
  { id: 'ai-skills', label: 'AI skills: list them only with evidence' },
  { id: 'traps', label: 'Fresher traps that cost interviews' },
  { id: 'ai-resumes', label: 'AI-written resumes and ATS myths' },
  { id: 'tailor-track', label: 'Tailor, follow up and track one number' },
  { id: 'beyond-resume', label: 'The resume is a means, not the goal' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
  { id: 'sources', label: 'Sources checked' },
];

const receiptSteps = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Name the tool or method exactly',
    body: 'Write "Excel pivot tables" or "Tally Prime", not "computer skills" or "MS Office". An exact name is easy to match against a job post and easy for you to defend.',
    note: 'If the job post says "Power BI", write "Power BI". Match the words only where they are true for you.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Say what you did with it, in one verb',
    body: 'Built, cleaned, reconciled, drew, wrote, tested, presented. The verb is what turns a word on a list into a thing you have done.',
    note: 'If you cannot start a sentence with a verb about this skill, you are not ready to list it yet. Learn it first, then list it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Attach an output someone can check',
    body: 'A file, a link, a drawing, a published page, a count, a name of a person who can confirm it. Not a feeling and not a percentage you cannot explain.',
    note: 'If there is no honest number, describe the change in plain words. An interviewer will ask you to explain any figure you print.',
  },
];

const salesRows = [
  {
    idea: 'Know who decides',
    resume: 'A recruiter scans first and a hiring manager checks later. The skills line must make sense to both: exact tool names for the recruiter, proof for the manager.',
  },
  {
    idea: 'Name the problem you solve',
    resume: 'Read the job post and find the three tasks it repeats. Your top skills should answer those three tasks, in the same words where true.',
  },
  {
    idea: 'Show proof',
    resume: 'One receipt line per core skill, plus one link to the work itself. A link beats a claim every time.',
  },
  {
    idea: 'Make a clear ask',
    resume: 'A headline that names the role you want, such as "Entry-level data analyst". A resume that asks for nothing gets nothing.',
  },
  {
    idea: 'Follow up',
    resume: 'After you apply, send one short note or ask for a referral. A resume that is never followed up works only as well as luck allows.',
  },
];

const atsRows = [
  {
    claim: 'Skills are the first thing recruiters filter on',
    evidence:
      'In a Jobscan survey of 384 recruiters (February to March 2025), 76.4% said they start filtering by skills, and over 99.7% use filters in their ATS. It is a vendor survey, so treat it as a strong hint, not a law.',
    action: 'Keep a clearly labelled "Skills" heading and use exact tool names.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'ATS software auto-rejects most resumes',
    evidence:
      'The "75% rejected by a robot" figure has no clear source. In Enhancv interviews with 25 US recruiters (2025), 23 said their system does not auto-reject for formatting or content. Two did use match thresholds. The sample is small.',
    action: 'Do not panic about a bot. Do expect hard knock-out questions on things like degree, location or work authorisation.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'More keywords means more chances',
    evidence:
      'Greenhouse, an ATS maker, warns that leaning too hard on keyword filters can overlook qualified people because of mismatched wording. A human still reads the shortlist, and a copied keyword list fails there.',
    action: 'Use the words of the job post only for skills you can discuss.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'Skill bars and star ratings are invisible to ATS',
    evidence:
      'Resume-tool vendors say so, but none of the pages we read published a test. The safe, tested-by-you method: paste your resume into a plain text file and see what survives.',
    action: 'Write skills as plain text. Ratings are weak anyway, because nobody knows what "4 out of 5" means.',
  },
  {
    claim: 'Recruiters read your resume for about seven seconds',
    evidence:
      'The Ladders 2018 eye-tracking study reported 7.4 seconds for the first screen. A recruiter-industry critic points out it measures the quick keep-or-skip look, not the full review.',
    action: 'Put your target role and best proof in the top third. Treat clear layout as the real lesson.',
  },
];

const backgrounds = [
  {
    name: 'BTech CSE or IT',
    scale: 'Scalable',
    scaleClass: 'high',
    why: 'Software and data skills can be sold directly as projects, freelance work or products. Warning: a degree alone is crowded. Campus shortlisting is one filter, and technical rounds are a second, separate one.',
    weak: 'Java, Python, C++, HTML, SQL, DBMS, OS, networking.',
    strong: 'Python with pandas: cleaned [number] rows of [a public dataset], found [one finding], and put the code and a README at [link].',
    core: 'One language used in one deployed project',
    multiplier: 'SQL, testing, or a domain such as finance or health',
    shelf: 'GitHub repository with a README, plus a live link',
  },
  {
    name: 'BTech non-CS (mechanical, civil, ECE, EEE)',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer',
    scaleClass: 'mid',
    why: 'Core jobs follow a fixed ladder. The business layer is a design, estimating, repair or automation service for small firms once you have real work to show.',
    weak: 'AutoCAD, SolidWorks, MATLAB, good knowledge of core subjects.',
    strong: 'AutoCAD: drew [number] sheets for [your final-year project], checked by [guide or site engineer], files kept at [link or folder].',
    core: 'The design or analysis tool your branch actually uses',
    multiplier: 'Excel automation, basic Python, or clear technical documentation',
    shelf: 'Drawing set, cost sheet or simulation report with one page of notes',
  },
  {
    name: 'BCom',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer',
    scaleClass: 'mid',
    why: 'Salaried accounts roles are a fixed ladder. Bookkeeping and compliance help for small shops and practices is the business layer.',
    weak: 'MS Office, Tally, good with numbers.',
    strong: 'Tally Prime: entered a month of [vouchers] for [a practice set or a family shop], matched the trial balance, and listed [the errors found].',
    core: 'Excel plus one accounting tool',
    multiplier: 'Power BI or plain business writing',
    shelf: 'Reconciliation sheet and one dashboard, with sensitive data removed',
  },
  {
    name: 'MBA',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer',
    scaleClass: 'mid',
    why: 'Management roles climb a ladder, and a general MBA is crowded. Consulting for small firms in one function (pricing, marketing, operations) is the business layer. An MBA does not replace proof.',
    weak: 'Leadership, strategic thinking, MS Excel, marketing.',
    strong: 'Excel and survey tools: sized the market for [a product] from [sources], and presented the result to [a company mentor], who [what they decided].',
    core: 'One business function, not "general management"',
    multiplier: 'Data analysis and a clear sales conversation',
    shelf: 'One-page case memo from a live project or internship',
  },
  {
    name: 'BA, arts and humanities',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer',
    scaleClass: 'mid',
    why: 'Writing, research and language skills scale when you own an audience or a portfolio. Content, research and communication work can be sold to small businesses directly.',
    weak: 'Good communication, content writing, creativity, MS Word.',
    strong: 'Content writing: wrote [number] explainers on [topic] for [college magazine or a small business], with [what happened, such as reads or a reply].',
    core: 'Writing or research, in one chosen topic',
    multiplier: 'Basic SEO, Canva or data reading',
    shelf: 'A simple writing portfolio page with five links',
  },
  {
    name: 'Diploma or ITI',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer',
    scaleClass: 'mid',
    why: 'Trade pay starts modest. Your own repair, installation or maintenance service is the business layer, and a job log with photos is the proof.',
    weak: 'Hands-on skills, machine operation, safety knowledge.',
    strong: 'Maintenance: serviced [machine type] using [tools], logged [fault] and [fix] in a job sheet, signed off by [supervisor].',
    core: 'The trade skill, with a written job log',
    multiplier: 'A digital estimate sheet, or supervision basics',
    shelf: 'Job log with photos and a supervisor reference',
  },
  {
    name: 'BSc (physics, chemistry, maths, biology, other)',
    scale: 'Scalable with a business layer when you add a data skill. Not so scalable as a lab-only or research-only route',
    scaleClass: 'low',
    why: 'Lab and research ladders are long and crowded, and few reach stable senior posts. A data, analysis or science-writing skill is what you can sell beyond them.',
    weak: 'Laboratory skills, research aptitude, MS Office.',
    strong: 'Spreadsheet analysis: analysed [your lab or survey data] with [method], and wrote a plain-English summary of [the result] for [a lecturer or a small client].',
    core: 'Lab method or maths, shown in one written report',
    multiplier: 'Excel or Python for data, or science communication',
    shelf: 'One report with the data, method and a one-page summary',
  },
];

const trapItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Skill bars, stars and percentages',
    body: 'Nobody knows what "80% Excel" means, and a low bar tells the employer to doubt you. Use plain words. If you must show level, use words such as "basic", "working" or "advanced", and be ready to prove the word.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'A keyword list copied from the job post',
    body: 'Copying 30 words from the post is the fastest way to be asked about the one you cannot explain. Keep only the words that are true.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Claims you cannot discuss',
    body: 'Every listed skill can become an interview question. If you could not talk about it for two minutes with an example, move it to a "learning" line or delete it.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Too many skills',
    body: 'Fresher guides suggest about 8 to 15 skills. Our working rule for one role is 8 to 12, because a long list hides the three that win the interview. This is a planning rule, not a law.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Certificates standing in for skills',
    body: 'A pile of course certificates is not a skill portfolio. List the course only next to the thing you built with it.',
  },
  {
    num: '06',
    title: 'Vague words like "hard-working" and "MS Office"',
    body: 'Hard-working is a personality claim, not a skill. "MS Office" is four tools. Say which one and what you did.',
  },
  {
    num: '07',
    title: 'One resume for every job',
    body: 'The skills line is the cheapest part to tailor. Reorder it so the three skills the post asks for come first.',
  },
];

const trackRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Applications sent',
    count: 'How many roles you applied to, and how many were a close fit.',
    meaning: 'Many applications with weak fit is spray and pray. Fewer, sharper applications are easier to learn from.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Replies',
    count: 'Replies of any kind: a call, an email, a test link, even a polite no.',
    meaning: 'Few replies usually point to role targeting, or to the top third of the resume not matching the post.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Interviews',
    count: 'Calls or meetings that came from your applications or referrals.',
    meaning: 'Good replies but few interviews often means the skills line has claims with no receipt.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Offers',
    count: 'Interviews that turned into an offer.',
    meaning: 'Interviews with no offers usually means you cannot yet explain your listed skills out loud.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What skills should a fresher put on a resume?',
    a: 'Put the skills the job post repeats and that you can show. For most freshers that is 8 to 12 items: a few exact tools (for example Excel, Tally or Python), two or three working methods (for example data cleaning or technical drawing), and one or two soft skills that you back with an example elsewhere on the resume. Skip anything you cannot discuss for two minutes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many skills should I list on my resume as a fresher?',
    a: 'Guides vary, from about 8 to 12 up to 10 to 15. We suggest 8 to 12 for one target role, grouped under two or three plain headings. The number matters less than this test: can you give a one-line example for each skill?',
  },
  {
    q: 'What to write in skills in resume when I have no experience?',
    a: 'Draw skills from what you have already done: college projects, assignments, internships, volunteering, a family business, or something you built on your own. Then use the receipt line: the exact tool, one verb for what you did, and an output someone can check.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I add soft skills to a fresher resume?',
    a: 'Yes, but not as a bare list. In Jobscan\'s recruiter survey, about half said hard and soft skills matter equally, and communication appears in 55% of job listings it analysed. A bare word proves nothing, so show it: "Presented [project] to [audience]; [what happened]". Put one or two soft skills in the skills line only if an example sits elsewhere on the page.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are good technical skills in resume for freshers?',
    a: 'The ones your target role uses daily and you have used for real: Excel and Tally for accounts, SQL and Python for data, a CAD tool for design, a language and Git for software. Name the tool, not the category. The best technical skill is the one with a project behind it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do skill bars or ratings help a fresher resume?',
    a: 'No. A rating is a score you gave yourself with no scale, and a low one signals doubt. Some vendors also say parsers cannot read graphics, though we found no published test. Plain text is safer on both counts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does ATS reject resumes because of the skills section?',
    a: 'Rarely on its own. In a small 2025 interview study of 25 recruiters, 23 said their system did not auto-reject for content or formatting. What recruiters do use are hard knock-out questions (such as degree, location or work authorisation) and filters on skills they search for. So the aim is to be findable and believable, not to beat a robot.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I list skills I am still learning?',
    a: 'Yes, in a separate, honest line such as "Currently learning: SQL (three practice queries on a public dataset)". Do not mix them with skills you have used. A learning line with real evidence beats a skill you cannot discuss.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I list AI tools like ChatGPT on my resume?',
    a: 'Only with an outcome. A US survey of 1,000 hiring managers reported by Built In found 78% called AI proficiency at least somewhat important, but only 19% very important, and 60% want to test or discuss it. Write what you made faster or better, how you checked the output, and name the tool inside that sentence.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is "MS Office" a skill to write on a resume?',
    a: 'It is too broad to carry weight. Write the part you used: Excel pivot tables, Word mail merge, PowerPoint for a client deck. Better still, attach one output, such as a dashboard or a report.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it OK to use AI to write my skills section?',
    a: 'Use it for wording and proofreading, not for inventing skills. In a 2025 TopResume survey of 600 US hiring managers, 52% accepted AI for proofreading or drafting help, and about 20% would reject a resume that looks AI-written. The bigger risk is listing something you then cannot explain.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should the skills section come before education on a fresher resume?',
    a: 'Usually yes. Put a short summary, then skills and projects, then education. If your degree itself is the filter for the job (a licensed field, for example), keep education higher. The aim is that your strongest proof sits in the top third of the page.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const sourceItems = [
  { href: 'https://www.jobscan.co/state-of-the-job-search/', label: 'Jobscan, The State of the Job Search in 2025 (recruiter survey of 384, 2.5 million applications; vendor data)' },
  { href: 'https://enhancv.com/blog/does-ats-reject-resumes/', label: 'Enhancv, ATS auto-rejection interviews with 25 US recruiters, 2025 (small vendor study)' },
  { href: 'https://www.greenhouse.com/blog/how-to-pick-a-candidate-centric-ats', label: 'Greenhouse, how to pick a candidate-centric ATS (keyword filtering caution)' },
  { href: 'https://www.hrdive.com/news/eye-tracking-study-shows-recruiters-look-at-resumes-for-7-seconds/541582', label: 'HR Dive, Ladders 2018 eye-tracking study (7.4 seconds)' },
  { href: 'https://www.ere.net/is-the-6-second-resume-scan-a-myth/', label: 'ERE, is the six-second resume scan a myth' },
  { href: 'https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined/', label: 'NACE, career readiness competencies (December 2025 version)' },
  { href: 'https://builtin.com/articles/show-ai-skills-resume', label: 'Built In, what AI skills managers look for on a resume (survey of 1,000 US hiring managers)' },
  { href: 'https://topresume.com/career-advice/ai-in-hiring-survey', label: 'TopResume, AI in hiring survey of 600 US hiring managers, 2025 (sponsored by a resume company)' },
  { href: 'https://www.mastersportal.com/articles/3420/skills-in-resume-for-freshers.html', label: 'Mastersportal, skills in resume for freshers (10 to 15 skills guidance)' },
  { href: 'https://www.hiration.com/blog/skill-bars-resume/', label: 'Hiration, do skill bars help your resume (vendor opinion, no published test)' },
  { href: 'https://news.careers360.com/india-skills-report-2026-employability-56-35-pc-ai-tools-digital-gig-economy-workforce-global-talent-hub', label: 'Careers360, India Skills Report 2026 coverage (employability 56.35%, 100,000+ test takers)' },
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="resume"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best skills for resume for freshers are the ones you can prove: an exact tool, what you did with it, and an output someone can check.</strong>
    A list of ten bare words ("Excel, communication, teamwork") is a list of claims, and a recruiter has no reason to believe any of them.
    A skills section that carries evidence helps you get past the first filter and, more important, helps you pick the roles that lead to a high-income skill portfolio and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The answer:</strong> list 8 to 12 skills that match the job post, name each tool exactly, and give every core skill one line with a tool, an action and a checkable output.</li>
      <li><strong>The filter:</strong> if you could not talk about a skill for two minutes with an example, it does not belong on the page yet.</li>
      <li><strong>The next step:</strong> pick your three strongest skills, write a receipt line for each, and move them to the top third of your resume.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Most fresher resumes lose at the skills line, not at the layout. The recruiter sees a long list copied from the job post, trusts none of it and moves on.
    Below you get the wording, the ATS facts we could verify, examples for seven degrees and the traps that quietly cost interviews.
    For the rest of the page, read <a href="/blog/resume/resume-tips-for-freshers-india/">resume tips for freshers in India</a>.
    For the summary line at the top, see <a href="/blog/resume/good-career-objective-for-resume/">a good career objective for a resume</a>.
    All three sit inside the <a href="/blog/resume/">resume library</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">What to write in skills in resume: the short answer</h2>

  <p>
    Write fewer skills and make each one checkable. A fresher has no job history, so the skills section is where your proof lives.
    It should answer one question for the person reading: "Can this person do the three things my job post asks for?"
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most guides tell freshers to add soft skills and match keywords, and stop there. That advice is true and not enough.
      Matching words gets you found. Only evidence gets you believed, and believed is what gets you the interview.
      A resume does not earn you the career. It earns you the conversation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="receipt-line">The receipt line: tool, action, checkable output</h2>

  <p>
    A receipt proves you bought something. A receipt line proves you did something.
    Every core skill on your resume should have one, either in the skills section or in a project or internship bullet beneath it.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {receiptSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="step__body">
          <div class="step__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="step__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    This is the first of The 3 Gates, <strong>Proof of Skill</strong>. The other two decide whether the skill turns into a job or paid work.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">The 3 Gates, applied to one line on your resume</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of Skill</span>
        <p>The receipt line: tool, action, checkable output. You can show the file or link.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of Communication</span>
        <p>You can explain that line aloud in 30 seconds to two minutes, in simple English, without reading it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of Value</span>
        <p>Three people, such as a lecturer, a mentor or a small business owner, have seen the work and can say it was useful.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Here is one skill moving from weak to strong. Replace the brackets with your real facts. Never copy a number.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Weak</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Good knowledge of MS Excel.</li>
        <li>Skills: communication, teamwork, MS Office, leadership.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">With a receipt</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Excel (pivot tables, XLOOKUP): built a [monthly expense tracker] for [your hostel mess or family shop]; file kept at [link].</li>
        <li>Presented [project name] to [who]; they [what they did next].</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="resume-as-sales">Your resume is a one-page sales document</h2>

  <p>
    Selling is not a bad word here. It means you know who decides, what problem they have, what proof you can show and what you are asking for.
    A resume that skips these is a list of facts about you. A resume that uses them is an offer.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Basic sales idea</th>
          <th>What it means on your resume</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salesRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Basic sales idea"><strong>{item.idea}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it means on your resume">{item.resume}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Who pays and how they trust you.</strong> For a job, the employer pays, and they trust a shortlisted resume plus a referral or a good interview.
      This route is employer-only, so the sales ideas above matter mainly for getting hired, and later for any side work you take on.
      Nobody can promise you a salary. What you control is how clearly your resume shows your proof.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="list-or-leave">What to list, what to leave out, and how many</h2>

  <p>
    Pick skills by asking three things: does the job post ask for it, have I used it, and can I show it? If any answer is no, it is a "learning" line at most, not a skill.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">List these</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Exact tools the post names, if you have used them (Excel, Tally, SQL, Figma, AutoCAD).</li>
        <li>Working methods tied to an output (data cleaning, reconciliation, technical drawing, copywriting).</li>
        <li>Languages you can actually work in, with an honest level in words.</li>
        <li>One or two soft skills only where an example sits elsewhere on the page.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Leave these out</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Anything you have only watched a video about and never used.</li>
        <li>Personality words: hard-working, honest, dedicated, quick learner.</li>
        <li>Skills nobody hires for in your target role (for a finance role, "Photoshop" with no link to the job).</li>
        <li>Skill bars, stars and percentages.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>How many?</strong> Fresher guides range from about 8 to 12 up to 10 to 15. Our rule of thumb for one role is 8 to 12, in two or three plain groups such as "Tools", "Methods" and "Languages".
    It is a planning rule, not a law. If the third group is thin, the list is probably padded.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>In what order?</strong> Put the skills the job post mentions most often first, inside each group. Lead with your strongest, because a quick scan can stop early.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ats-truth">How ATS and recruiters treat skills, claim by claim</h2>

  <p>
    There is a lot of folklore here. We checked what we could and marked what is vendor opinion. Treat every number as a hint from a small or sponsored study unless it says otherwise.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Common claim</th>
          <th>What the evidence shows</th>
          <th>What to do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {atsRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Common claim"><strong>{item.claim}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the evidence shows">{item.evidence}</td>
            <td data-label="What to do">{item.action}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    One more honest gap: how a given ATS weighs a skill that appears both in a skills list and in a project bullet is not publicly documented for most products.
    So do both: list it in the skills line and prove it in a bullet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="soft-skills">Soft skills and communication in English: why they need proof</h2>

  <p>
    Employers do want soft skills. NACE, the US career-readiness body, lists communication, critical thinking, teamwork, professionalism and technology among its competencies.
    In Jobscan's analysis of 4.6 million job listings, communication appeared in 55% of them.
    Among 384 recruiters, about half said hard and soft skills matter equally.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is exactly why the bare word fails. Every fresher writes "communication". It only counts when something shows it.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The claim</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Good communication skills.</li>
        <li>Team player.</li>
        <li>Fluent in English.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The evidence</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Wrote and sent [number] client emails during [internship], with [what came back].</li>
        <li>Coordinated [a team of number] for [a college event or project], and [what was delivered].</li>
        <li>English: presented [project] in English to [audience]; [a short recorded explanation at a link].</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    English deserves its own line of thought. It is a multiplier skill, because it widens which roles, referrals and mentors you can reach. It is not a measure of how clever you are.
    If English is your weak spot, do not hide it. Record a one-minute explanation of one project, listen, rewrite it and record again. That recording is also proof you can share.
    You can think in one language and sell your work in another, and that is a fair way to improve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="by-background">Before and after skill lines by degree</h2>

  <p>
    Seven backgrounds are below. They are ordered by how directly the skill can be sold outside one employer, so the most scalable options come first.
    The labels use our scale: Scalable, Scalable with a business layer, and Not so scalable.
  </p>

  <p>
    A resume gets you hired. The skill underneath is what can grow later. Replace every bracket with your own facts, and do not print a number you cannot explain.
  </p>

