  <h2 id="core-multiplier">Core skill, multiplier skill and a visible proof shelf</h2>

  <p>
    Ten skills with no depth look the same as ten claims. A high-income skill portfolio starts smaller. Build a stack instead: one core skill that the role cannot live without, one multiplier that makes the core more valuable, and one place where the proof sits.
  </p>

  <div class="stack-cards">
    <div class="stack-card stack-card--teal">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Core skill</div>
      <p>The thing the role is hired for. Example for an accounts role: Tally and Excel reconciliation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card stack-card--gold">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Multiplier skill</div>
      <p>The thing that lifts the core. Example: a Power BI dashboard, or clear email writing to clients.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-card stack-card--violet">
      <div class="stack-card__label">Proof shelf</div>
      <p>One place the evidence lives and a stranger can open: GitHub, a dashboard link, a writing page, a drawing folder, a job log.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Pick one shelf and build it well before opening a second. One shelf with three real pieces beats five empty profiles.
    A school-leaver can show a tiny project. A graduate can show an internship result or a first paid task. If your field is confidential, such as accounts for a firm, use sanitised samples, a practice set or a written process note.
  </p>

  <p>
    The information to build these skills is mostly free: YouTube, official documentation, free practice datasets and low-cost courses.
    The test of any course is not the certificate. It is what you built with it, and who looked at it and gave you feedback.
    If you can only afford one thing, spend your time on the project, not on the course list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-skills">AI skills on a fresher resume: list them only with evidence</h2>

  <p>
    AI fluency is a real skill, and it is also the easiest one to fake. In the Built In survey of 1,000 US hiring managers, 78% said AI proficiency matters at least somewhat, but only 19% called it very important, and it ranked sixth among the skills they weigh.
  </p>

  <p>
    About 60% said they want to test it, discuss it or see proof. That is a warning: if you list a tool you cannot use, the interview exposes it.
  </p>

  <p>
    India's own signal points the same way: the India Skills Report 2026 coverage says AI, data analytics, cloud and cybersecurity are the most in-demand skills.
    Demand is real. So is the filter that follows it.
  </p>

  <div class="mini-grid">
    <div class="mini-card">
      <div class="mini-card__label">Understand</div>
      <p>Know what the tool does and where it gets things wrong.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mini-card">
      <div class="mini-card__label">Use</div>
      <p>Apply it to a real task in your field, not a toy prompt.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mini-card">
      <div class="mini-card__label">Check</div>
      <p>Verify the output against a source and note what you corrected.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mini-card">
      <div class="mini-card__label">Create</div>
      <p>Make something better with it and explain how.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Risky</div>
      <ul>
        <li>AI tools: ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Midjourney.</li>
        <li>Prompt engineering expert.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Believable</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Used an AI assistant to draft [number] survey questions for [project], checked each against [source], corrected [number] errors, and sent the final form to [number] people.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A staged way to build this: begin with one assisted task in your own field and keep notes on what you fixed.
    Later, as you learn more, turn the repeat task into a small workflow you can explain and, if it fits your field, offer to others.
    Treat AI as leverage on top of a core skill, not as a replacement for having one.
    The pressure is specific: first drafts, basic data cleaning and routine formatting are now quick for anyone with a tool, so a line that shows only that work stands out less. A line that shows your checking and judgment stands out more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="traps">Fresher traps that cost interviews</h2>

