  <h2 id="ai-resumes">AI-written resumes and ATS myths: an honest note</h2>

  <p>
    Using AI to polish a resume is normal. Using it to invent a resume is the trap.
    In the TopResume survey of 600 US hiring managers (a resume company sponsored it, so weigh it with care), 52% were fine with AI for proofreading or drafting help, and about 20% would reject a resume that looks fully AI-written.
    Only 33.5% of them correctly spotted the AI-written samples when tested, so detection is far from perfect, but the reaction is real when it happens.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The bigger cost of an AI-padded skills list is not a bot or a rejection email. It is the interview where you cannot explain the third skill on your list.
      Write the first draft yourself from your own work, then use AI to tighten the wording. And do not trust any page, ours included, that promises you a "100% ATS score". No ATS publishes how it scores a fresher resume.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tailor-track">Tailor, follow up and track one number</h2>

  <p>
    Tailoring does not mean rewriting everything. Reorder the skills line so the three skills the post stresses come first, swap in the post's exact tool names where true, and check that your top bullet matches the role.
    In Jobscan's own data on 2.5 million applications, a resume title that matched the job title went with a much higher interview rate, though that shows a link, not a cause.
  </p>

  <p>
  </p>

  <p>
    Then follow up. After you apply, find one person in that company or from your college and send a short, specific message: one thing you noticed about their work, one link to your proof, one small question. Ask for a referral only after you have given them something useful to read.
  </p>

  <p>
    Enhancv's recruiter interviews also suggest that applying early helps, since recruiters review in arrival order and may stop once they have enough strong candidates. That came from 25 recruiters, so take it as a tip, not a rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Track one number so you stop guessing. It is the path from applications to replies to interviews.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>What to count</th>
          <th>What a weak number usually means</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {trackRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Stage"><strong>{item.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What to count">{item.count}</td>
            <td data-label="What a weak number usually means">{item.meaning}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    We do not publish a "good" rate for each stage because we have no reliable Indian figure for freshers. Compare yourself with yourself: change one thing, send a fresh batch of applications, and see which number moves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beyond-resume">The resume is a means, not the goal</h2>

  <p>
    A resume is a way to get a first conversation with an employer. The skill underneath it is worth more than the paper.
    The same receipt lines, proof shelf and short pitch you built here can be reused when you want to take a small freelance or consulting task, because they answer the same question for any client: "Can you do this, and can I check?"
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable with a business layer.</strong> Skills such as data analysis, bookkeeping, writing, design or technical drawing can start as a job and later be sold as a small paid service.
      The business layer is a clear offer ("monthly books for small shops"), proof others can check, a way to follow up and ask for referrals, and an honest price conversation. It takes time, it is not guaranteed, and it works only if the underlying skill is real.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    This is also why <strong>skills first, degree second</strong> is the right order for most careers. A degree still matters for licensed professions and some employers. But a degree without a visible skill is what the next 200 resumes also have.
    The decisions that unlock high-income opportunities usually come from the skill stack you build alongside it.
  </p>

